# The scope of a negation is the part of the sentence that `not`
# denies. In `all nodes do not run the agent`, `not` can deny `all`, so
# that some nodes run it, or it can deny `run` for each node, so that
# none do. `Not all nodes run the agent` and `no node runs the agent`
# each read one way.
#
# The rule counts `all` or `both`, then a noun, an adjective or a noun
# before a noun, or `of them`, `of these`, or `of those`, and then a
# plural verb such as `are`, `do`, or `can` before `not`. Only plural
# verbs count, because a singular verb means that `all` sits in a
# phrase of its own (`a check across all claims does not run`). `each`
# stays out, because `each file is not cached` reads only as none.
#
# It hit 3 lines in the measure.py corpus, and all 3 meant none.
expr main ([all, both] [<NOUN, !PRON>, (<[NOUN, ADJ, PROPN], !PRON> NOUN), (of [them, these, those])] [are, do, were, have, can, will, should, must] not)
let message "Negation scope. Write not all or none, whichever the sentence means."
let description "Flags all and both before a negated plural verb, where not can deny all or each one."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Not all nodes run the agent."
allows "None of the nodes run the agent."
allows "A check across all claims does not run."
